# PROJECT: ESP32 Wi-Fi ACCESS POINT — INTERACTIVE SIMULATION LAB

## 1. YOUR ROLE

Act as a senior embedded systems engineer, ESP32 firmware developer, Wi-Fi networking engineer, simulation software architect, and expert frontend developer.

Build a complete, functional, interactive educational simulation website that demonstrates exactly how an ESP32 microcontroller operates in Wi-Fi Access Point (AP) mode.

This is not a static website, a presentation, a documentation page, or a collection of pictures.

It must be a working browser-based virtual laboratory in which users can interact with an ESP32, start and stop its Wi-Fi access point, connect simulated client devices, observe networking events, inspect IP addresses, simulate data transmission, and learn how each operation works internally.

The interface must be extremely minimal, professional, technically accurate, and easy for an electronics engineering student to understand.

Do not ask me questions. Make sensible engineering decisions and build the complete application.

## 2. MAIN OBJECTIVE

Create a virtual laboratory that simulates this complete communication flow:

ESP32 → Creates Wi-Fi Access Point → Broadcasts SSID → Client Scans Networks → Client Connects → Authentication → Association → DHCP IP Assignment → Data Communication → Client Disconnects.

The user must be able to observe the entire process step by step and also run it automatically.

The simulation must explain what the ESP32 does, what the client device does, what happens over Wi-Fi, and what occurs at the IP networking layer.

Important technical distinction:
The browser simulation must not pretend that it creates a real Wi-Fi access point or transmits real radio packets. All network operations are simulated in software unless a separate, explicitly implemented hardware integration is available.

